lse/crop/8667x4875+0+446/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2Ff5%2F14%2Fc41a211243cca4495a4be2641da4%2Fgettyimages-2306544716.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8667x4875+0+446/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2Ff5%2F14%2Fc41a211243cca4495a4be2641da4%2Fgettyimages-2306544716.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8667x4875+0+446/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2Ff5%2F14%2Fc41a211243cca4495a4be2641da4%2Fgettyimages-2306544716.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8667x4875+0+446/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2Ff5%2F14%2Fc41a211243cca4495a4be2641da4%2Fgettyimages-2306544716.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8667x4875+0+446/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2Ff5%2F14%2Fc41a211243cca4495a4be2641da4%2Fgettyimages-2306544716.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8667x4875+0+446/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2Ff5%2F14%2Fc41a211243cca4495a4be2641da4%2Fgettyimages-2306544716.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8667x4875+0+446/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2Ff5%2F14%2Fc41a211243cca4495a4be2641da4%2Fgettyimages-2306544716.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8667x4875+0+446/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2Ff5%2F14%2Fc41a211243cca4495a4be2641da4%2Fgettyimages-2306544716.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8667x4875+0+446/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2Ff5%2F14%2Fc41a211243cca4495a4be2641da4%2Fgettyimages-2306544716.jpg" class="img" alt="A sign hangs from a fence surrounding the Iowa Army Ammunition Plant, on Nov. 23, 2026, in Middletown, Iowa." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 A sign hangs from a fence surrounding the Iowa Army Ammunition Plant, on Nov. 23, 2026, in Middletown, Iowa.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Scott Olson/Getty Images                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 &lt;strong&gt;The U.S. Army rescheduled the detonation multiple times after &lt;/strong&gt;the public got wind of it last week, NPR's Quil Lawrence tells &lt;em&gt;Up First&lt;/em&gt;. Purdue University's Andrew Whelton, who has worked on disaster planning, tells Lawrence the detonation caused stress for residents, businesses and farmers. The Trump administration pushed back on the notion that this planned action was a surprise, citing town hall meetings on the topic. Lawrence says the &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6053785:x" target="_blank"&gt;biggest danger in the detonation&lt;/a&gt; is to kids and pregnant people due to the lead dust in the air or on surfaces. There are also worries about potential harm to livestock and crops, as well as the cleanup duration.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Kilmar Abrego Garcia has voluntarily left the U.S. after a nearly two-year legal battle. &lt;/strong&gt;He is the Salvadoran immigrant who became emblematic of the challenges posed by President Trump's mass deportation campaign. Abrego Garcia was arrested in 2025 and mistakenly deported to El Salvador. He returned to the U.S. but departed for Spain yesterday. His lawyer, Simon Sandoval-Moshenberg, said that Abrego Garcia boarded a commercial flight to a destination of his choice and "&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/11/25/nx-s1-6053203/abrego-garcia-leaves-us" target="_blank"&gt;left the United States a free man&lt;/a&gt;." Sandoval-Moshenberg described the move as "a temporary departure," saying that Abrego Garcia, whose wife and child are U.S. citizens, has options to return to the U.S.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 &lt;strong&gt;Abrego Garcia's attorney said that he will essentially spend two years waiting abroad.&lt;/strong&gt; His case raises concerns about due process and the risks of sending individuals to &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6053785:x" target="_blank"&gt;countries where they could be harmed&lt;/a&gt;, NPR's Juliana Kim says. The Department of Homeland Security said they are delighted by his decision to leave the U.S. The agency, in a statement, said that "now this El Salvadoran man cannot terrorize innocents." After being mistakenly deported, upon his return to the U.S., Abrego Garcia was charged with human smuggling, an allegation he has denied.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;       &lt;aside id="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" class="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" aria-label="recommended stories"&gt;&lt;/aside&gt;    &lt;!-- END ID="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" CLASS="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" ARIA-LABEL="RECOMMENDED STORIES" --&gt;    &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Nvidia's newly reinstated CEO, Jensen Huang, apologized to his staff&lt;/strong&gt; yesterday for his previous behavior, according to the &lt;em&gt;New York Times.&lt;/em&gt; He reportedly denied that Trump pressured the board for his return, despite the president saying otherwise. Huang had stepped down following reports of affairs, including one with a woman reportedly suspected by U.S. intelligence agencies of being recruited by China's spy agency. Some members of Congress still have concerns about the national security scandal.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 &lt;strong&gt;Some lawmakers are upset with Huang&lt;/strong&gt;, while others criticized Nvidia and its board, NPR's Maham Javaid says. Democratic Sen. Elizabeth Warren says that Huang owes Congress answers about Nvidia's compliance with laws designed to keep advanced chips out of China. Republican Sen. Tom Cotton of Arkansas is calling for a congressional investigation into Huang and urging faster action on chip-control legislation. House Democratic Committee leaders sent a letter to the president after he claimed he helped with Huang's reinstatement. They asked if he &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6053785:x" target="_blank"&gt;prioritizes friends and allies over&lt;/a&gt; the nation's economic and national security interests. They demanded a response by Dec. 8 on what he knew about Huang's ties to an alleged Chinese spy. Huang will attend a closed-door hearing with House members next week, followed by testimony before a Senate committee two days later.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Today's listen&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-136166" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 3000;         --source-height: 1688;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fed%2F31%2Fdfaeac5542c286a2b92070f67baf%2Ftalanis-spc-final.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fed%2F31%2Fdfaeac5542c286a2b92070f67baf%2Ftalanis-spc-final.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fed%2F31%2Fdfaeac5542c286a2b92070f67baf%2Ftalanis-spc-final.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fed%2F31%2Fdfaeac5542c286a2b92070f67baf%2Ftalanis-spc-final.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fed%2F31%2Fdfaeac5542c286a2b92070f67baf%2Ftalanis-spc-final.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fed%2F31%2Fdfaeac5542c286a2b92070f67baf%2Ftalanis-spc-final.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fed%2F31%2Fdfaeac5542c286a2b92070f67baf%2Ftalanis-spc-final.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fed%2F31%2Fdfaeac5542c286a2b92070f67baf%2Ftalanis-spc-final.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fed%2F31%2Fdfaeac5542c286a2b92070f67baf%2Ftalanis-spc-final.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fed%2F31%2Fdfaeac5542c286a2b92070f67baf%2Ftalanis-spc-final.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fed%2F31%2Fdfaeac5542c286a2b92070f67baf%2Ftalanis-spc-final.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fed%2F31%2Fdfaeac5542c286a2b92070f67baf%2Ftalanis-spc-final.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fed%2F31%2Fdfaeac5542c286a2b92070f67baf%2Ftalanis-spc-final.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fed%2F31%2Fdfaeac5542c286a2b92070f67baf%2Ftalanis-spc-final.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fed%2F31%2Fdfaeac5542c286a2b92070f67baf%2Ftalanis-spc-final.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fed%2F31%2Fdfaeac5542c286a2b92070f67baf%2Ftalanis-spc-final.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fed%2F31%2Fdfaeac5542c286a2b92070f67baf%2Ftalanis-spc-final.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fed%2F31%2Fdfaeac5542c286a2b92070f67baf%2Ftalanis-spc-final.jpg" class="img" alt="NPR Option 2" loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;NPR launched the first-ever Senior Podcast Challenge this fall, inviting people over 65 to share their stories. Unlike the NPR Student Podcast Challenge, which has attracted more than 90,000 young voices since 2018, this contest focuses on the wisdom and experiences of older voices. While the contest is still open for a few more days, the judges have already listened to more than 100 submissions. Some stories explore life after retirement, dating after divorce or the loss of a partner, and coming to terms with being a senior. Take a listen to some of their &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/11/22/nx-s1-6044221/npr-senior-podcast-challenge-early-entries" target="_blank"&gt;favorite early entries&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;p&gt;There's still time to enter. The competition will close on Nov. 30. These are the &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/08/16/nx-s1-5929441/npr-senior-podcast-challenge-official-rules" target="_blank"&gt;official rules&lt;/a&gt; for the 2026 NPR Senior Podcast Challenge and you can &lt;a href="https://npr.formstack.com/forms/senior_podcast_challenge" target="_blank"&gt;submit your entry here&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Life advice&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-154742" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 4356;         --source-height: 2450;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4356x2450+0+227/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F3e%2F55%2F5987a64f424b84b128aba38ba342%2Fnpr-lr-stop-thinking-about-work-harlan-3.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4356x2450+0+227/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F3e%2F55%2F5987a64f424b84b128aba38ba342%2Fnpr-lr-stop-thinking-about-work-harlan-3.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4356x2450+0+227/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F3e%2F55%2F5987a64f424b84b128aba38ba342%2Fnpr-lr-stop-thinking-about-work-harlan-3.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4356x2450+0+227/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F3e%2F55%2F5987a64f424b84b128aba38ba342%2Fnpr-lr-stop-thinking-about-work-harlan-3.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4356x2450+0+227/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F3e%2F55%2F5987a64f