e/crop/8091x4551+0+421/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9e%2F96%2Fe23d590f45f8bbd605875a794fb7%2Fgettyimages-2304485779.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8091x4551+0+421/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9e%2F96%2Fe23d590f45f8bbd605875a794fb7%2Fgettyimages-2304485779.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8091x4551+0+421/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9e%2F96%2Fe23d590f45f8bbd605875a794fb7%2Fgettyimages-2304485779.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8091x4551+0+421/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9e%2F96%2Fe23d590f45f8bbd605875a794fb7%2Fgettyimages-2304485779.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8091x4551+0+421/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9e%2F96%2Fe23d590f45f8bbd605875a794fb7%2Fgettyimages-2304485779.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8091x4551+0+421/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9e%2F96%2Fe23d590f45f8bbd605875a794fb7%2Fgettyimages-2304485779.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8091x4551+0+421/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9e%2F96%2Fe23d590f45f8bbd605875a794fb7%2Fgettyimages-2304485779.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8091x4551+0+421/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9e%2F96%2Fe23d590f45f8bbd605875a794fb7%2Fgettyimages-2304485779.jpg" class="img" alt="A person looks at a departures board showing delays and cancellations at LaGuardia airport on November 13, 2026 in the Queens borough of New York City." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 A person looks at a departures board showing delays and cancellations at LaGuardia airport on November 13, 2026 in the Queens borough of New York City.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Adam Gray/Getty Images North America                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 &lt;strong&gt;Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy said that the FAA will reevaluate&lt;/strong&gt; future maintenance procedures, NPR's Joe Hernandez tells &lt;em&gt;Up First&lt;/em&gt;. Mike McCormick, a former FAA senior executive and air traffic manager, told Hernandez that he thinks another nationwide ground stop is unlikely. McCormick says that in cases like this, the FAA sometimes &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6043667:x" target="_blank"&gt;imposes a maintenance moratorium&lt;/a&gt;, temporarily halting such work. The ground stop happened while L3Harris workers converted copper wires to a fiber optic system and installed a new router in El Dorado, Arkansas.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;President Trump is in Manila today to meet with allies at the ASEAN summit. &lt;/strong&gt;Tomorrow, he travels to Shenzhen, China, for a joint summit with Chinese leader Xi Jinping and Russian President Vladimir Putin. It's the first time those three have had a joint summit. The meeting will happen on the sidelines of the APEC summit that Xi is hosting in Shenzhen.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 &lt;strong&gt;When leaving the White House yesterday, Trump said they would discuss &lt;/strong&gt;his "favorite subject": Ukraine, NPR's Deepa Shivaram says. The White House had previously announced that Ukraine would &lt;em&gt;not&lt;/em&gt; be on the agenda for the trilateral meeting. Shivaram says that Europe and Ukraine are concerned about &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6043667:x" target="_blank"&gt;how Ukraine may be discussed&lt;/a&gt; at the meeting, and what might come out of it, because President Volodymyr Zelenskyy will not be at APEC and Trump has recently been more lenient toward Putin and Russia.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang's resignation has shaken Silicon Valley&lt;/strong&gt;, prompting calls from some tech leaders for the board to reinstate him. Trump said he agreed with them. Meanwhile, the U.S. government introduced a rule to &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/11/16/nx-s1-6042883/nvidia-jensen-huang-commerce-department" target="_blank"&gt;block remote access to advanced AI chips&lt;/a&gt; and servers, closing a loophole that could allow adversarial governments to access American AI technology through overseas data centers. Nvidia is the world's dominant producer of AI chips.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 &lt;strong&gt;Many prominent AI and venture capital figures have recognized Huang as a visionary&lt;/strong&gt; whose company played a significant role in the AI boom, NPR's Shannon Bond says. An online petition supporting his reinstatement has &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6043667:x" target="_blank"&gt;garnered more than 160,000 signatures&lt;/a&gt;, but Bond says some of them may not be real. Elon Musk says he signed it. Brad Gerstner from Altimeter Capital, a key Nvidia investor, has urged the board to thoroughly investigate and keep all options open. Bond says the interim CEO and CFO will address the public for the first time following today's earnings report.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;       &lt;aside id="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" class="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" aria-label="recommended stories"&gt;&lt;/aside&gt;    &lt;!-- END ID="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" CLASS="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" ARIA-LABEL="RECOMMENDED STORIES" --&gt;    &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Prosecutors are moving to retry Lindsay Clancy for the deaths of her three children. &lt;/strong&gt;The new &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/11/16/nx-s1-5983473/clancy-trial-retrial-murder-charges" target="_blank"&gt;trial is set for March 29, 2027&lt;/a&gt;. Although there was speculation about potentially reducing the charges to manslaughter or second-degree murder after her previous trial ended in a mistrial, no changes were made during Monday's court hearing. Judge William Sullivan, who presided over the first trial, will oversee the new proceedings. He denied Clancy's defense team's request to dismiss the charges based on double jeopardy. Clancy's defense argues that she shouldn't be held criminally responsible for killing her children because she was suffering from severe postpartum psychosis.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Deep dive&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-153583" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 7440;         --source-height: 4185;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcd